// An alias defined in terms of itself, which names no type.
//
// An alias is a binding: a local spelling and a target, and every reader walks
// the chain of them to whatever is at its end. A chain that comes back to itself
// has no end, so the cycle is reported and cut where the aliases are resolved,
// before anything follows one (design/phases/names-and-namespaces.md, "Aliases").
//
// Three shapes, because each is a different length of chain: an alias of itself,
// a pair naming each other, and a longer ring. One diagnostic per ring, at
// whichever name the walk reached first: cutting the cycle there ends the chain
// for every other name in it, so the rest name 'unknown' rather than each
// earning a report of their own.
//
// This file replaced the one that pinned an alias failing as the base of a path.
// That failure was a consequence of a typedef being its own kind of node: the
// collapse found the typedef, which is neither a module nor a type, and gave up.
// An alias answers for its target, so the collapse now walks through to the
// struct and the path resolves -- pinned as working in typemgmt-success.

typedef Loop Loop;              //~ ErrorCircular:9 "defined in terms of itself"

typedef Ping Pong;              //~ ErrorCircular:9 "defined in terms of itself"
typedef Pong Ping;

typedef Ring1 Ring2;            //~ ErrorCircular:9 "defined in terms of itself"
typedef Ring2 Ring3;
typedef Ring3 Ring1;
